1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:00,962
TL;DR.

2
00:00:00,962 --> 00:00:03,087
This week in 30 seconds:.

3
00:00:03,087 --> 00:00:16,425
Building freeze: New York becomes the first US state to impose a one-year moratorium on new data centers of 50 megawatts or more — electricity prices have risen 68% since 2019 .

4
00:00:16,425 --> 00:00:26,150
Power bloc: Xi Jinping launches WAICO, a new AI alliance with 29 founding states, positioned as a counterweight to the US-led AI order .

5
00:00:26,150 --> 00:00:38,425
Arms race: Moonshot AI's Kimi K3, with 2–3 trillion parameters, is set to catch up with Anthropic's Opus 4.8 — open weights expected on July 27 .

6
00:00:38,425 --> 00:00:49,900
Blind spot: Anthropic's own analysis of 309,815 conversations shows Claude responds with varying warmth or rigor depending on the input language .

7
00:00:49,900 --> 00:00:51,150
Story of the Week.

8
00:00:51,150 --> 00:00:54,375
New York halts the data center boom — for one year.

9
00:00:54,375 --> 00:01:08,400
A state that has so far welcomed every hyperscaler with open arms is hitting the brakes: Governor Kathy Hochul has signed a statewide, one-year moratorium on new "hyperscale" data centers requiring 50 megawatts or more of power .

10
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:13,487
New York is the first US state to freeze AI construction plans of this scale .

11
00:01:13,487 --> 00:01:28,475
The trigger isn't abstract AI skepticism — it's the electricity bill: statewide power prices have risen 68% since 2019, and concern over grid capacity is growing faster than new transmission lines can be built .

12
00:01:28,475 --> 00:01:38,387
Unlike earlier local pushback against individual construction projects, this is the first statewide, government-level intervention into the US data center boom.

13
00:01:38,387 --> 00:01:56,762
Why it matters: For cloud providers and AI labs, this signals a measurable shift in the data center site-selection race toward states with looser energy policy — and for New York consumers, it's a rare case where electricity-price concerns actually carry political weight against the AI infrastructure lobby.

14
00:01:56,762 --> 00:02:07,125
Critical voices: Direct statements from supporters or opponents of the moratorium were not accessible at the time of research (the source page blocked automated access).

15
00:02:07,125 --> 00:02:23,150
The tech industry can generally be expected to argue that such a building freeze weakens competitiveness in the global AI race and shifts investment to other states or countries — but no official statements from Amazon, Microsoft, Google, or Meta are available yet.

16
00:02:23,150 --> 00:02:30,862
Open questions: how the affected hyperscalers will actually respond, and whether other states follow suit, remain unclear.

17
00:02:30,862 --> 00:02:39,125
Bottom line: Rising electricity prices have just become the first serious political brake on US AI infrastructure expansion.

18
00:02:39,125 --> 00:02:40,462
More Top Stories.

19
00:02:40,462 --> 00:02:44,287
Gemini 3.5 Pro reaches general availability.

20
00:02:44,287 --> 00:02:51,375
Google has officially brought Gemini 3.5 Pro to general availability after roughly a six-week delay .

21
00:02:51,375 --> 00:03:02,725
New features include a context window expanded to 2 million tokens and a new "Deep Think" reasoning mode, available in the $250/month Ultra subscription .

22
00:03:02,725 --> 00:03:14,900
The delay itself is notable: a six-week slip on a flagship model suggests Google is under heightened pressure in the current model race — though the available sources don't give a concrete reason for the delay.

23
00:03:14,900 --> 00:03:15,725
So what?

24
00:03:15,725 --> 00:03:29,075
Anyone planning production workflows on Gemini 3.5 Pro can now count on the wider context window and the new reasoning mode, but given the long delay should plan a careful independent evaluation before rollout.

25
00:03:29,075 --> 00:03:34,700
Kimi K3: China's largest open model takes aim at Opus 4.8.

26
00:03:34,700 --> 00:03:52,150
Moonshot AI is preparing to release Kimi K3 — an open-weight, mixture-of-experts model with 2 to 3 trillion parameters and a 1-million-token context window, reportedly expected to perform "at par with or even surpass Anthropic's Opus 4.8" .

27
00:03:52,150 --> 00:03:55,875
It's considered the largest open-weight model out of China to date .

28
00:03:55,875 --> 00:04:00,987
The open weights are expected to be released on July 27, 2026 .

29
00:04:00,987 --> 00:04:18,112
Critical context: Open weights from Chinese models routinely raise questions for Western companies around training-data provenance, possible export-control implications, and safety or censorship bias — but Moonshot has not yet provided concrete details on any of this for Kimi K3.

30
00:04:18,218 --> 00:04:18,993
So what?

31
00:04:18,993 --> 00:04:35,243
For teams betting on open weights over proprietary APIs, Kimi K3 could become a serious alternative to Western frontier models with comparable context length starting late July — provided the provenance and safety questions are acceptable for the intended use case.

32
00:04:35,243 --> 00:04:40,318
Xi Jinping launches WAICO: China's counter-model to the AI world order.

33
00:04:40,318 --> 00:04:54,855
For the first time, Xi Jinping personally delivered the opening address at the World AI Conference in Shanghai — accompanied by the founding of the "World Artificial Intelligence Cooperation Organisation" (WAICO) with 29 founding states .

34
00:04:54,855 --> 00:04:58,255
UN Secretary-General Guterres was also present .

35
00:04:58,255 --> 00:05:11,630
Xi explicitly positioned the initiative as a multilateral counter-model to the US-led AI order: "AI development should not be a solo performance by a single country, but a symphony of international cooperation" .

36
00:05:11,630 --> 00:05:21,493
At the same time, he stressed that "AI is always under human control" and warned against overstretching the concept of national security in the AI domain .

37
00:05:21,493 --> 00:05:35,143
Critical context: China itself runs one of the world's strictest censorship and surveillance infrastructures in the AI domain — Xi's call for "international cooperation" sits in tension with its own domestic practice.

38
00:05:35,143 --> 00:05:43,330
Statements from Western governments or US-led AI alliances on the founding of WAICO were not yet available at the time of research.

39
00:05:43,330 --> 00:05:44,155
So what?

40
00:05:44,155 --> 00:05:56,918
With 29 founding states — mostly from the Global South — China is building its own standardization and cooperation framework that directly competes with Western governance initiatives like the AI Safety Summit.

41
00:05:56,918 --> 00:05:57,868
Quick Hits.

42
00:05:57,868 --> 00:05:58,943
Briefly noted:.

43
00:05:58,943 --> 00:06:10,555
Anthropic: Claude for Teachers offers verified US K-12 teachers free premium access, curriculum-aligned across all 50 states, with a pilot launch in Detroit .

44
00:06:10,555 --> 00:06:20,780
Worth noting critically: as an Anthropic in-house product, independent assessments of student data-privacy implications and statements from teacher unions are still missing.

45
00:06:20,780 --> 00:06:34,055
Apple: China's Cyberspace Administration has approved Apple Intelligence with local partners (Alibaba's Qwen for text/image, Baidu for search) — Apple remains the only foreign provider on the approval list .

46
00:06:34,055 --> 00:06:50,205
Microsoft: "Project Perception" combines Anthropic, OpenAI, and in-house models into a cheaper security tool for vulnerability detection — the main argument against Anthropic's Mythos is price, whose API costs are reportedly roughly double those of Opus .

47
00:06:50,205 --> 00:06:51,330
Tool of the Week.

48
00:06:51,330 --> 00:06:58,168
Gemini Notebook (formerly NotebookLM) — Google's research tool gets a new name and a new core feature.

49
00:06:58,168 --> 00:07:09,168
Google is fully rebranding NotebookLM as "Gemini Notebook," tying it more closely to the Gemini brand, including a planned integration into Search's AI Mode .

50
00:07:09,168 --> 00:07:17,993
New is a cloud compute environment that runs code directly inside notebooks — a secure cloud environment for data analysis in the Pro tier .

51
00:07:17,993 --> 00:07:28,580
Over 30 million users and 600,000+ organizations already use the tool; old NotebookLM links and the domain remain functional .

52
00:07:28,580 --> 00:07:36,543
Especially useful for teams that want to combine research notes with real code execution without setting up a separate analysis environment.

53
00:07:36,543 --> 00:07:44,318
The Gemini 3.5 and Antigravity upgrade is announced to arrive for AI Pro subscribers in the coming weeks .

54
00:07:44,318 --> 00:07:45,330
View the tool

55
00:07:45,330 --> 00:07:46,480
Fail of the Week.

56
00:07:46,480 --> 00:07:51,355
"Claude is warmer in Hindi and stricter in English — and Anthropic didn't know"

57
00:07:51,355 --> 00:08:10,843
Anthropic's own analysis of 309,815 anonymized Claude.ai conversations reveals systematic, unintended value differences: depending on input language and model, Claude responds either warmer or stricter — Hindi triggers the warmest responses, English the strictest .

58
00:08:10,843 --> 00:08:23,668
Notably, the models studied (Sonnet 4.6, Opus 4.6/4.7) have since been superseded by Sonnet 5 — the inconsistency only became visible in retrospect .

59
00:08:23,786 --> 00:08:33,736
Context: The fact that Anthropic published the study itself is not reassuring on its own — it mainly means the only available data comes from the company under scrutiny.

60
00:08:33,736 --> 00:08:46,323
Independent reviews of the methodology or comparison data for competing models (GPT, Gemini) are still lacking, so it's not yet possible to tell whether the issue is Claude-specific or industry-wide.

61
00:08:46,323 --> 00:09:08,423
Root cause: The value axes (warmth vs. rigor, restraint vs. caution) drift apart depending on the distribution of training data per language, without any single training step specifically optimizing against it — a side effect of unevenly distributed multilingual training corpora that only becomes visible through after-the-fact conversation analysis .

62
00:09:08,423 --> 00:09:21,023
What we're learning: Anyone running a model in production across multiple languages should evaluate behavior separately per language — an eval pass in English says nothing about behavior in Hindi, Arabic, or German.

63
00:09:21,023 --> 00:09:22,161
Number of the Week.

64
00:09:22,161 --> 00:09:25,023
309,815

65
00:09:25,023 --> 00:09:33,511
That's how many anonymized Claude.ai conversations Anthropic analyzed for its study on language- and model-dependent value differences .

66
00:09:33,511 --> 00:09:48,898
Compared to classic red-teaming samples of a few hundred to a few thousand examples, this scale shows how systematically Anthropic now analyzes real user behavior — and how easily biases of this kind can still slip through production unnoticed for months .

